# Playwright TypeScript Best Practices

## Project Layout

```
tests/          # Spec files — orchestrate flows, keep thin
pages/          # Page Object classes — UI locators & actions
services/       # API service classes — HTTP calls & data setup/teardown
fixtures/       # Custom test fixtures (optional)
```

## Locators

Prefer user-facing locators: `getByRole`, `getByLabel`, `getByPlaceholder`, `getByText`, `getByTestId`. Avoid CSS/XPath unless no semantic option exists.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
await page.locator('.btn-primary').click();

// ✅ GOOD
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Submit' }).click();
```

## Assertions

Use web-first `expect` matchers — they auto-retry. Never assert on raw boolean checks.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
expect(await page.getByText('Welcome').isVisible()).toBe(true);

// ✅ GOOD
await expect(page.getByText('Welcome')).toBeVisible();
```

## Page Object Model

One class per page or major UI section. Colocate locators and user actions; keep assertions in tests.

```typescript
// pages/login.page.ts
import { type Locator, type Page } from '@playwright/test';

export class LoginPage {
  readonly emailInput: Locator;
  readonly submitButton: Locator;

  constructor(readonly page: Page) {
    this.emailInput = page.getByLabel('Email');
    this.submitButton = page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' });
  }

  async goto() {
    await this.page.goto('/login');
  }

  async signIn(email: string, password: string) {
    await this.emailInput.fill(email);
    await this.page.getByLabel('Password').fill(password);
    await this.submitButton.click();
  }
}
```

POM rules:
- Expose `readonly` locators; define them in the constructor.
- Methods represent user actions (`signIn`, `addToCart`), not test steps.
- Return `this` or a related page object for fluent navigation when useful.
- Do not import `test` or `expect` inside page objects (except rare internal waits).
- Instantiate directly in tests — no fixture wrapper needed for simple `new LoginPage(page)`.

```typescript
test('user can sign in', async ({ page }) => {
  const loginPage = new LoginPage(page);
  await loginPage.goto();
  await loginPage.signIn('user@test.com', 'secret');
});
```

## API Services

When tests need API setup, teardown, or verification, wrap `APIRequestContext` in a service class — never scatter raw HTTP calls in specs.

```typescript
// services/users.service.ts
import { type APIRequestContext } from '@playwright/test';

export class UsersService {
  constructor(private readonly request: APIRequestContext) {}

  async createUser(data: { email: string; name: string }) {
    const response = await this.request.post('/api/users', { data });
    await response.ok(); // throws on failure
    return response.json();
  }

  async deleteUser(id: string) {
    await this.request.delete(`/api/users/${id}`);
  }

  async getUser(id: string) {
    const response = await this.request.get(`/api/users/${id}`);
    await response.ok();
    return response.json();
  }
}
```

Service rules:
- One service per API domain (`UsersService`, `OrdersService`).
- Use `request.post/get/patch/delete` with typed request/response shapes.
- Call `await response.ok()` or assert status explicitly — do not ignore failures.
- Set `baseURL` and auth headers in `playwright.config.ts` under `use.extraHTTPHeaders` — Playwright's built-in `request` fixture picks them up automatically.
- Use services for preconditions (seed data) and postconditions (verify server state); keep UI assertions in tests.
- Instantiate services directly — Playwright already injects `request`; wrapping it in a custom fixture is redundant unless you need shared setup/teardown.

```typescript
test('created user appears in admin list', async ({ page, request }) => {
  const usersService = new UsersService(request);
  const adminUsersPage = new AdminUsersPage(page);

  const user = await usersService.createUser({ email: 'a@test.com', name: 'Alice' });
  await adminUsersPage.goto();
  await expect(adminUsersPage.rowFor(user.email)).toBeVisible();
});
```

### When custom fixtures ARE worth it

Skip fixtures for thin `new Service(request)` wrappers. Add them only when they do real work:

- **Auth setup** — create a browser context with `storageState` before each test
- **Lifecycle** — `beforeAll`/`afterAll` for a shared API context that must be disposed
- **Composition** — many test files import one extended `test` with 5+ shared dependencies
- **Config overrides** — different fixture values per Playwright project (admin vs user role)

## Test Structure

- One behavior per test; descriptive names.
- Specs orchestrate page objects and services — no duplicated selectors or HTTP paths.
- Keep tests independent; clean up created data in `test.afterEach` via services.
- Always `await` Playwright API calls.

## Stability & Config

- No arbitrary `waitForTimeout` — rely on auto-waiting and web-first assertions.
- Use `test.step()` for multi-step flows.
- Set `baseURL` in `playwright.config.ts`; use relative paths in POMs and services.
- Type config with `defineConfig`; read secrets from env, never hardcode.
